<DocsLayout title="Notifications" activeNav="/platform/integrations/notifications">
  <h1>Notifications</h1>
  <p>
    Notification destinations turn platform events into messages in a chat channel. Unlike webhooks,
    which hand a payload to your own code, this is the built-in path: pick the events, write the
    message once, and route it to Slack, Discord, or Telegram.
  </p>

  <Screenshot
    src="integrations-notifications"
    alt="Notifications tab with two destinations, provider selection, channel label, stored webhook URL, notification tags and per-event toggles"
    caption="The routing model: event, then template, then destination."
  />

  <h2>Event, template, destination</h2>
  <p>
    The three chips in the header describe the whole model. A <strong>destination</strong> subscribes
    to <strong>events</strong>, and each subscribed event has its own <strong>template</strong> — the
    message body that gets sent. Nothing is delivered for an event with no template, which is why the
    events list and the templates are edited together on one screen.
  </p>

  <h2>Configuring a destination</h2>
  <Screenshot
    src="notifications-destination-form"
    alt="Destination form showing provider selector, name, channel label, credential field, and active toggle"
    caption="The form adapts its fields to the selected provider."
  />
  <p>
    Select a destination from the left sidebar to edit it, or click <strong>New destination</strong>
    to create one. The right panel shows the configuration form with three sections: settings,
    events, and templates.
  </p>

  <h3>Settings</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Field</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Provider</td><td>Slack, Discord, or Telegram — determines the credential field and message limits.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Name</td><td>A recognizable label for the destination (e.g. <code>#mobile-releases</code>).</td></tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Channel label</td>
        <td>
          For Slack and Discord: a display label only — the webhook URL decides the actual channel.
          For Telegram: the numeric chat ID or <code>@channel</code> username used when sending.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Credential</td>
        <td>
          Slack/Discord: incoming webhook URL. Telegram: bot token. Encrypted at rest and never shown
          again after save. Leave blank to keep the current value.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr><td>Active / Paused</td><td>Disables delivery without deleting the destination or its templates.</td></tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Notification tags</td>
        <td>
          Tags inserted into templates via <code>&#123;&#123;tags&#125;&#125;</code> or
          <code>&#123;&#123;tags_str&#125;&#125;</code>. A property of the destination, not of the build.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Providers</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Provider</th><th>Credential</th><th>Message limit</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Slack</td><td>Webhook URL (<code>hooks.slack.com</code>)</td><td>4 000 chars</td><td>Webhook determines the channel</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Discord</td><td>Webhook URL (<code>discord.com</code>)</td><td>2 000 chars</td><td>Webhook determines the channel</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Telegram</td><td>Bot token</td><td>4 000 chars</td><td>Chat ID or <code>@channel</code> required</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Switching provider on a saved destination shows a warning: the stored credential is replaced, so
    the old provider stops receiving messages. The form offers to create a separate destination
    instead.
  </p>

  <h2>Events and templates</h2>
  <Screenshot
    src="notifications-template-editor"
    alt="Template editor showing event list with toggles, message textarea, insertable variable chips, and rendered example preview"
    caption="Select an event from the left list to edit its message template on the right."
  />
  <p>
    The left column lists all available events grouped by category (Builds, Credentials, etc.).
    Toggle an event on to subscribe to it, then click its name to edit the message template. The
    right column shows the template textarea, insertable variable chips, and a rendered example
    preview.
  </p>

  <h3>Available events</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Category</th><th>Event</th><th>Fires when</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td rowspan="2">Application</td><td><code>mobile_app.created</code></td><td>A new application is added</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mobile_app.deleted</code></td><td>An application is removed</td></tr>
      <tr><td rowspan="3">Identifiers</td><td><code>app_identifier.created</code></td><td>A platform identifier is added</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>app_identifier.updated</code></td><td>An identifier or its version is changed</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>app_identifier.deleted</code></td><td>An identifier is removed</td></tr>
      <tr><td rowspan="4">Builds</td><td><code>build.created</code></td><td>A build enters the queue</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>build.started</code></td><td>A runner starts working on a build</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>build.completed</code></td><td>A build succeeds, fails, or is cancelled</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>artifact.created</code></td><td>A downloadable artifact is attached</td></tr>
      <tr><td rowspan="8">Credentials</td><td><code>credential.created</code></td><td>A credential is added to the vault</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>credential.updated</code></td><td>A credential or its assignments change</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>credential.deleted</code></td><td>A credential is removed</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>credential.expiring_30_days</code></td><td>30 days before expiration</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>credential.expiring_7_days</code></td><td>7 days before expiration</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>credential.expiring_1_day</code></td><td>24 hours before expiration</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>credential.expired</code></td><td>Credential has expired</td></tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>credential.verification_failed</code></td>
        <td>A build could not resolve a credential referenced in an external vault</td>
      </tr>
      <tr><td>Team Management</td><td><code>team.invitation.created</code></td><td>Someone is invited to the workspace</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    There is no <code>build.failed</code> event: subscribe to <code>build.completed</code> and use
    <code>&#123;&#123;build.status&#125;&#125;</code> in the template, which covers all final states
    (completed, failed, cancelled).
  </p>

  <h3>Template variables</h3>
  <p>
    Templates use double-braced, dotted placeholders. Click a variable chip below the textarea to
    insert it. Variables with no value render as <code>—</code> rather than an empty gap.
  </p>
  <p>
    Application metadata and build Launch parameters support arbitrary dotted paths. For example,
    <code>&#123;&#123;app.metadata.owner.name&#125;&#125;</code> reads a nested metadata field and
    <code>&#123;&#123;build.parameters.flags.minify&#125;&#125;</code> reads a nested Launch parameter.
    Use <code>&#123;&#123;app.metadata_str&#125;&#125;</code> or
    <code>&#123;&#123;build.parameters_str&#125;&#125;</code> to insert the complete object as compact JSON.
    Objects and arrays selected by a path also render as compact JSON; booleans render as
    <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Launch parameters remain encrypted in storage and excluded from webhook payloads and audit
    values. A notification template that references them deliberately sends the selected values to
    the destination, so use these placeholders only with trusted channels — especially
    <code>&#123;&#123;build.parameters_str&#125;&#125;</code>, which sends the complete object.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Each event exposes its own set, and a variable outside that set is rejected on save</strong>
    rather than rendering as a literal <code>&#123;&#123;…&#125;&#125;</code> in a live message. The
    chips under the textarea are the authoritative list for the event you have selected; the tables
    below say which events each variable belongs to. Examples match the values the
    <strong>Rendered example</strong> panel fills in.
  </p>

  <h4>Application events</h4>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Variable</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;app.name&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Application display name</td><td>Atlas Mobile</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;app.slug&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Application URL slug</td><td>atlas-mobile</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;app.description&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Description, when available (created only)</td><td>Customer mobile application</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;app.url&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Direct link to the application (created only)</td><td>https://…/apps/atlas-mobile</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;actor.name&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Who created or deleted it</td><td>Maya Chen</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;app.tags&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>App tags prefixed with #</td><td>#ios #production</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;app.tags_str&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>App tags as plain text</td><td>ios production</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;app.metadata.*&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Any application metadata value at a dotted path</td><td>Mobile Platform</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;app.metadata_str&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Complete application metadata as compact JSON</td><td>&#123;&quot;tier&quot;:&quot;production&quot;&#125;</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h4>Identifier events</h4>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Variable</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;app.name&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>The application this identifier belongs to</td><td>Atlas Mobile</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;identifier.value&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Bundle ID, package name, or web domain</td><td>com.example.atlas</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;identifier.platform&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Identifier platform</td><td>ios</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;identifier.name&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Optional display name</td><td>Atlas Mobile iOS</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;identifier.version&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Configured version, when available</td><td>4.8.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;identifier.url&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Link to the identifier settings</td><td>https://…/identifiers/ios</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;actor.name&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Who made the change</td><td>Maya Chen</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;app.tags&#125;&#125;</code>, <code>&#123;&#123;app.tags_str&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>App tags, with and without <code>#</code></td><td>#ios #production</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;app.metadata.*&#125;&#125;</code>, <code>&#123;&#123;app.metadata_str&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Application metadata by path or as complete JSON</td><td>production</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h4>Build events</h4>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Variable</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;app.name&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Application name</td><td>Atlas Mobile</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;build.number&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Workspace build sequence number</td><td>482</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;build.profile&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Build profile (created and started only)</td><td>production</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;build.requested_by&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Who requested it (created only)</td><td>Maya Chen</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;build.branch&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Source branch or ref (started only)</td><td>release/4.8</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;build.status&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Final status (completed only)</td><td>completed</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;build.status_icon&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Icon matched to the final status (completed only)</td><td>✅</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;build.duration&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Human-readable duration (completed only)</td><td>8m 42s</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;build.url&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Direct link to the build</td><td>https://…/builds/482</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;build.tags&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Build tags prefixed with #</td><td>#release #hotfix</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;build.tags_str&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Build tags as plain text</td><td>release hotfix</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;build.parameters.*&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Any Launch parameter at a dotted path</td><td>internal</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;build.parameters_str&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Complete Launch parameters as compact JSON</td><td>&#123;&quot;channel&quot;:&quot;internal&quot;&#125;</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;app.metadata.*&#125;&#125;</code>, <code>&#123;&#123;app.metadata_str&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Application metadata by path or as complete JSON</td><td>production</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <code>&#123;&#123;build.profile&#125;&#125;</code> is the one that catches people: it is not
    available on <code>build.completed</code>, so a "build finished" template has to name the profile
    some other way or leave it out.
  </p>

  <h4>Artifact events</h4>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Variable</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;app.name&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>The application the artifact belongs to</td><td>Atlas Mobile</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;artifact.name&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>File name</td><td>atlas-4.8.0.ipa</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;artifact.type&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>File format</td><td>ipa</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;artifact.size&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Human-readable size</td><td>54.2 MB</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;artifact.download_url&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Direct download link</td><td>https://…/atlas-4.8.0.ipa</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;build.number&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>The source build sequence number</td><td>482</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;build.tags&#125;&#125;</code>, <code>&#123;&#123;build.tags_str&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Build tags, with and without <code>#</code></td><td>#release #hotfix</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;build.parameters.*&#125;&#125;</code>, <code>&#123;&#123;build.parameters_str&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Source build Launch parameters by path or as complete JSON</td><td>internal</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;app.metadata.*&#125;&#125;</code>, <code>&#123;&#123;app.metadata_str&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Application metadata by path or as complete JSON</td><td>production</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h4>Credential events</h4>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Variable</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;credential.name&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Credential display name</td><td>App Store Certificate</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;credential.type&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Credential category</td><td>ios_certificate</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;credential.expires_at&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Expiration date, when available</td><td>Aug 31, 2026</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;credential.url&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Link to Credentials Vault</td><td>https://…/credentials</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;app.name&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>The app linked to this credential, when any</td><td>Atlas Mobile</td></tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>&#123;&#123;credential.days_remaining&#125;&#125;</code></td>
        <td>The reminder milestone — expiry events only (<code>0</code> on <code>credential.expired</code>)</td>
        <td>7</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>&#123;&#123;actor.name&#125;&#125;</code></td>
        <td>Who made the change. <strong>Not available on the four expiry events</strong> — nobody performs an expiry.</td>
        <td>Maya Chen</td>
      </tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;credential.tags&#125;&#125;</code>, <code>&#123;&#123;credential.tags_str&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Credential tags, with and without <code>#</code></td><td>#ios #certificate</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;app.metadata.*&#125;&#125;</code>, <code>&#123;&#123;app.metadata_str&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Metadata for the first linked application, when any</td><td>production</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <code>credential.verification_failed</code> carries the same set as the created, updated and
    deleted events, with <code>&#123;&#123;actor.name&#125;&#125;</code> naming what reported the
    failure rather than a person.
  </p>

  <h4>Team invitation</h4>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Variable</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;team.name&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Workspace display name</td><td>Atlas Team</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;invitation.email&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Invited email address</td><td>alex@example.com</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;invitation.role&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Role granted by the invitation</td><td>contributor</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;invitation.url&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Direct link to accept it</td><td>https://…/invitations/accept</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;actor.name&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Who sent the invitation</td><td>Maya Chen</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The invitation URL is an acceptance link. A destination subscribed to this event puts that link
    into a chat channel, so subscribe it only where the channel membership is as trusted as the
    workspace itself.
  </p>

  <h4>Global variables (available in every template)</h4>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Variable</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;tags&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Destination tags prefixed with #</td><td>#mobile-releases</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;tags_str&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Destination tags as plain text</td><td>mobile-releases</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    These two are the destination's own tags, not the build's or the app's — a build tag reaches a
    template through <code>&#123;&#123;build.tags&#125;&#125;</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>Rendered example</h3>
  <p>
    Below the template textarea, a <strong>Rendered example</strong> panel shows how the message
    will look with real values filled in. The <strong>Send example</strong> button delivers a test
    message to the destination so you can verify the webhook works before going live.
  </p>

  <h2>Failures</h2>
  <p>
    Destinations track consecutive failures and the last delivery time, and those changes show up in
    the <a href="/platform/audit-log">audit log</a> — the entries reading
    <code>Failures: 2 → 3</code> come from here. A destination whose webhook URL was revoked in Slack
    keeps failing silently otherwise, so that counter is the signal worth watching.
  </p>

  <h2>Console-only, by omission rather than by design</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Notification destinations have no API.</strong> Their routes live in the
    session-authenticated web application, and <code>routes/api.php</code> defines nothing for them,
    so there is no API v1 endpoint and no CLI command — not even through
    <code>appmctl request</code>, which is scoped to <code>/api/v1/&#123;team&#125;/</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you need chat notifications under version control or created by a script, use
    <a href="/platform/integrations/webhooks">webhooks</a> and post to chat from your own receiver.
    That trades the built-in templates for control over both the message and where its configuration
    lives. The same point, from the CLI's side, is on
    <a href="/cli/integrations/notifications">the CLI notifications page</a>.
  </p>
